gmann S., Over H., Ertl G., J. Phys. Chem. 101, 11185 (1997).","journal-title":"J. Phys. Chem."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_7_2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"6267","DOI":"10.1021\/jp990503v","volume":"103","author":"B\u00f6ttcher A.","year":"1999","unstructured":"B\u00f6ttcher A., Rogazia M., Niehus H., Over H., Ertl G., J. Phys. Chem. 103, 6267 (1999).","journal-title":"J. Phys. Chem."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_8_2","unstructured":"V. E. Henrich and P. A. Cox The Surface Science of Metal Oxides (Cambridge Univ. Press Cambridge 1996)."},{"key":"#cr-split#-e_1_3_1_9_2.1","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","unstructured":"G. A. Somorjai Introduction to Surface Chemistry and Catalysis (Wiley New York 1994)","DOI":"10.1146\/annurev.pc.45.100194.003445"},{"key":"#cr-split#-e_1_3_1_9_2.2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","unstructured":"G. Ertl H. Kn\u00f6zinger J. Weitkamp Eds. Handbook of Heterogeneous Catalysis (Wiley New York 1997).","DOI":"10.1002\/9783527619474"},{"key":"e_1_3_1_10_2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"1","DOI":"10.1016\/S0360-0564(08)60268-0","volume":"20","author":"Burwell R. L.","year":"1969","unstructured":"Burwell R. L., Haller G. L., Taylor K. C., Read J. F., Adv. Catal. 20, 1 (1969).","journal-title":"Adv. Catal."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_11_2","unstructured":"F. Bruno A. Morgante H. Over unpublished results."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_12_2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","unstructured":"M. A. Van Hove W. H. Weinberg C.-M. Chan Low-Energy Electron Diffraction (Springer-Verlag Berlin 1985);","DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-642-82721-1"},{"key":"e_1_3_1_12_3","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"637","DOI":"10.1088\/0034-4885\/58\/6\/003","volume":"58","author":"Heinz K.","year":"1995","unstructured":"Heinz K., Rep. Prog. Phys. 58, 637 (1995);","journal-title":"Rep. Prog. Phys."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_12_4","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"249","DOI":"10.1016\/S0079-6816(98)00029-X","volume":"58","author":"Over H.","year":"1998","unstructured":"Over H., Prog. Surf. Sci. 58, 249 (1998).","journal-title":"Prog. Surf. Sci."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_13_2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"495","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevLett.78.495","volume":"78","author":"Charlton G.","year":"1997","unstructured":"Charlton G., et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 78, 495 (1997).","journal-title":"Phys. Rev. Lett."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_14_2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","unstructured":"For the DFT calculations we used the generalized gradient approximation of J. P. Perdew et al. [ Phys. Rev. B 46 6671 (1992)] for the exchange-correlation functional and used ab initio pseudopotentials created by the scheme of N. Troullier and J. L. Martins [ Phys. Rev. B 43 1991 (1993)] in the fully separable form. The Ru core is taken to consist of all orbitals up to and including 4 p states and O cores consist of the 1 s state. The electronic wave functions were expanded in a plane-wave basis set. The surface was modeled by five independent layers of RuO 2 (110) in a symmetric slab (supercell approach) [","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevB.43.1993"},{"key":"e_1_3_1_14_3","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevB.46.16067"},{"key":"e_1_3_1_14_4","unstructured":"]; by mirror plane operation nine layers per RuO 2 (110) slab in total are generated. Consecutive RuO 2 (110) slabs are separated by a vacuum region of about 16 \u00c5 which was checked to be sufficient to avoid interaction between the RuO 2 (110) slabs. Calculations were performed with a (1 \u00d7 1) surface unit cell with an energy cut-off of 60 Ry. The integral over the Brillouin zone was performed by using a special k-point set by S. L. Cunningham [ Phys. Rev. B 10 4988 (1974)] with 8 k-points in the irreducible part of the (1 \u00d7 1) Brillouin zone. To accelerate the electronic relaxation Fermi broadening of the occupation numbers was used with a width of 0.1 eV and the energies were extrapolated to zero temperature. The calculation scheme allowed for the relaxation of the electrons and atoms. We relaxed all O and Ru coordinates which maintain the planar 2-mm symmetry. Only the positions of the central Ru + O layer lying on the mirror plane of the symmetric RuO 2 (110) slab were frozen in. The lattice parameters (2a = 6.58 \u00c5 and c = 3.23 \u00c5) of RuO 2 (110) were determined by DFT optimization of the corresponding bulk RuO 2 the values of which compare reasonably well with experimental ones of 2a = 6.38 \u00c5 and c = 3.11 \u00c5. For further details of the program code see A. P. Seitsonen thesis Technical University Berlin (2000) and www.iki.fi\/aps\/Thesis.html."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_15_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevB.60.14396"},{"key":"e_1_3_1_16_2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"315","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevLett.70.315","volume":"70","author":"Over H.","year":"1993","unstructured":"Over H., Moritz W., Ertl G., Phys. Rev. Lett. 70, 315 (1993).","journal-title":"Phys. Rev. Lett."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_17_2","unstructured":"For CO on Ru(0001) the theoretical value is 1.8 eV. CO desorption takes place at around 480 K ["},{"key":"e_1_3_1_17_3","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"4613","DOI":"10.1063\/1.446378","volume":"79","author":"Pfn\u00fcr H.","year":"1983","unstructured":"Pfn\u00fcr H., Feulner P., Menzel D., J. Chem. Phys. 79, 4613 (1983)].","journal-title":"J. Chem. Phys."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_18_2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"2772","DOI":"10.1021\/j100792a006","volume":"68","author":"Blyholder G.","year":"1964","unstructured":"Blyholder G., J. Phys. Chem. 68, 2772 (1964).","journal-title":"J. Phys. Chem."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_19_2","unstructured":"This assignment differs from the clean TiO 2 (110) surface but it turned out that imaging of bridging O atoms is sensitively affected by the state of the STM tip ["},{"key":"e_1_3_1_19_3","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/S0039-6028(98)00356-2"},{"key":"e_1_3_1_20_2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"41","DOI":"10.1016\/S0009-2509(54)80005-4","volume":"3","author":"Mars P.","year":"1954","unstructured":"Mars P., van Krevelen D. W., Chem. Eng. Sci. 3, 41 (1954).","journal-title":"Chem. Eng. Sci."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_21_2","unstructured":"Recent experiments in which a O + CO covered Ru(0001) surface was irradiated by infrared femtosecond laser pulses demonstrated that this obstacle may be overcome by weakening the Ru\u2013O bond through transiently reaching high electron temperatures before CO desorption is completed by phonon excitation ["},{"key":"e_1_3_1_21_3","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1126\/science.285.5430.1042"},{"key":"e_1_3_1_22_2","unstructured":"Under similar tunneling conditions adsorbed O atoms in the Ru(0001)-(2\u00d72)-O phase were previously unequivocally identified with dark spots in the STM image ["},{"key":"e_1_3_1_22_3","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"159","DOI":"10.1016\/S0039-6028(97)00604-3","volume":"394","author":"Wintterlin J.","year":"1997","unstructured":"Wintterlin J., et al., Surf. Sci. 394, 159 (1997)].","journal-title":"Surf. Sci."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_23_2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"226","DOI":"10.1002\/ijch.197700037","volume":"16","author":"Hirschfeld F. L.","year":"1977","unstructured":"Hirschfeld F. L., Isr. J. Chem. 16, 226 (1977);","journal-title":"Isr. J. Chem."},{"key":"e_1_3_1_23_3","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"1732","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevB.47.1732","volume":"47","author":"Glassford K. M.","year":"1993","unstructured":"Glassford K. M., Chelikowsky J. R., Phys. Rev. B 47, 1732 (1993).","journal-title":"Phys. Rev. B"},{"key":"e_1_3_1_24_2","unstructured":"H.O. thanks the surface science group for the warm hospitality at the Technische Universit\u00e4t Vienna during the STM measurements. A.M. acknowledges partial financial support by the Max-Planck-Gesellschaft during his 6-month visit at the Fritz-Haber-Institut. The work in Vienna was supported by the Fonds zur F\u00f6rderung der wissenschaftlichen Forschung and E.L. was supported by th